.TH "VIRTUALBRICKS\-ARCHIVE" "7" "2026\-10\-05" "virtualbricks 3.0.0.dev2" "Virtualbricks Manual"
.SH NAME
virtualbricks\-archive \- project archives, and the process that reads
and writes them
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[I]project\f[R].vbp
.PP
\f[B]python \-m virtualbricks.config.archive\f[R] \f[B]<\f[R]
\f[I]job.toml\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Virtualbricks exports a project to an archive, a \f[I].vbp\f[R] file,
and imports a project from one, on the same computer or on another.
The archive holds the project file, its README, the private disks of the
virtual machines and, if you choose, the disk images they are based on:
often gigabytes.
.PP
So the application never reads or writes an archive itself.
For each job, inspecting an archive, exporting a project or importing
one, it starts a process of its own, the \f[I]archive process\f[R].
It writes the job to the process\(aqs standard input, as TOML, and
follows the job on the process\(aqs standard output, one JSON object a
line, while the interface stays responsive.
The process runs one job and exits.
It runs at a lower priority, \f[B]nice\f[R] 10, and it can be stopped at
any time with SIGTERM.
.IP
.EX
  application                         archive process
 +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+                      +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
 |            |\-\- stdin: the job \-\-\->|             |
 | ArchiveJob |   TOML, then EOF     |   main()    |\-> bsdtar
 |            |                      |             |   or tar
 |            |<\- stdout: messages \-\-|             |
 |            |   JSON, one a line   |             |\-> qemu\-img
 |            |<\- stderr \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|             |
 |            |   kept for errors    |             |
 |            |\-\- SIGTERM: cancel \-\->|             |
 |            |<\- exit status \-\-\-\-\-\-\-|             |
 +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+                      +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.PP
This page describes the format of the archives (\f[B]THE ARCHIVE\f[R]),
the protocol between the application and the process (\f[B]THE
PROCESS\f[R]), the three jobs (\f[B]JOBS\f[R]), the progress they report
(\f[B]PROGRESS\f[R]), the tools they run (\f[B]ARCHIVE TOOLS\f[R]) and
how the application drives them (\f[B]THE APPLICATION\(aqS SIDE\f[R]).
It\(aqs for whoever works on that code, and for anyone who wants to run
the process by hand, to look into an archive or to script an export.
.SH THE ARCHIVE
.SS Format
An archive is a tar file in the POSIX.1\-2001 format, \f[I]pax\f[R],
with the extension \f[I].vbp\f[R].
When \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] is found, the archive is not compressed as a
whole: its disks are compressed one by one inside it (see \f[B]Packed
disks\f[R]).
Without \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R], the whole archive is compressed with gzip.
.PP
Virtualbricks reads archives that are not compressed or compressed with
gzip, bzip2 or xz, whatever their extension, so an archive made by hand
with \f[B]tar\f[R](1) can be imported too.
The Import window offers the files \f[I]*.vbp\f[R], \f[I]*.tar.gz\f[R],
\f[I]*.tgz\f[R] and \f[I]*.tar\f[R].
.SS Members
Every member is a regular file, named by its path relative to the
project\(aqs folder.
A reader drops a leading \f[I]./\f[R] and a trailing \f[I]/\f[R] from
the names.
The name gives the kind of the member, the word in italics, which the
process uses in its messages (see \f[B]inspect\f[R]):
.TP
\f[B]contents.toml\f[R]
\f[I]contents\f[R]: the list of the members; see \f[B]contents.toml\f[R]
below.
.TP
\f[B]project.toml\f[R]
\f[I]project\f[R]: the project file, described in
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5).
.TP
\f[B].project\f[R], \f[B].project\(ti\f[R]
\f[I]legacy project\f[R]: the project file of Virtualbricks 2.1 and
older.
It is converted as the migration does.
.TP
\f[B]README.md\f[R], \f[B]README\f[R]
\f[I]readme\f[R]: the description of the project, plain text in UTF\-8
that Virtualbricks reads as Markdown (see \f[B]README\f[R] in
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5)).
The archive keeps it as it is.
Before 3.0 it was \f[B]README\f[R], which the import renames to
\f[B]README.md\f[R]; in an archive with both, \f[B]README.md\f[R] is the
README.
.TP
\f[I]vm\f[R]_\f[I]device\f[R].cow
\f[I]disk\f[R]: the private disk of the device \f[I]device\f[R], as
\f[B]hda\f[R] or \f[B]vdb\f[R], of the virtual machine \f[I]vm\f[R].
Only at the top of the archive; \f[I]device\f[R] has only lowercase
letters and digits.
.TP
\&.images/\f[I]image\f[R]
\f[I]image\f[R]: the disk image called \f[I]image\f[R] in the project
file.
The member is named after the image, not after its file: an image
\f[B]debian\f[R] whose file is \f[I]/srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2\f[R] is
stored as \f[I].images/debian\f[R].
.TP
any other name
\f[I]other\f[R]: a file of the project\(aqs folder that you chose to
export.
The import puts it back where it was.
.SS Order
A new archive starts with \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], then the project file
and the README: together, the \f[I]head\f[R] of the archive.
The other files, the private disks and the images follow, in this order,
each group from the smallest file to the largest.
An inspection reads the head and stops there, without reading the
gigabytes that follow it.
.IP
.EX
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  | contents | project | README | other | disks | .images/  |
  |   .toml  |  .toml  |  .md   | files |       |           |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  |<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- head \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|<\-\-\- smallest to largest \->|
         read by inspect               read by import
.EE
.SS contents.toml
The list of the other members, in the order of the archive.
It is TOML 1.0, as the files described in
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5):
.IP
.EX
format = 1

[[members]]
name = \(dqproject.toml\(dq
size = 255

[[members]]
name = \(dqREADME.md\(dq
size = 16

[[members]]
name = \(dqvm1_hda.cow\(dq
size = 197120
packed = true
real_size = 196616

[[members]]
name = \(dq.images/debian\(dq
size = 197120
packed = true
real_size = 196616
.EE
.TP
\f[B]format\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R]
The version of the layout, \f[B]1\f[R].
An archive with a higher \f[B]format\f[R] is not read:
\(dqcontents.toml: written by a newer Virtualbricks (format 2)\(dq.
.TP
\f[B]members\f[R] = \f[I]array of tables\f[R]
One table for each member but \f[B]contents.toml\f[R] itself:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
The name of the member.
.TP
\f[B]size\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R]
Its size in the archive, in bytes.
.TP
\f[B]packed\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R]
Present, and \f[B]true\f[R], only when \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] compressed
the member.
.TP
\f[B]real_size\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R]
Only with \f[B]packed\f[R]: its size before it was compressed, the size
it has on the computer it comes from.
.RE
.PP
The kind of a member comes from its name and is not written.
An item of \f[B]members\f[R] that isn\(aqt a table is skipped, a size
that isn\(aqt an integer is read as 0, and other keys are ignored.
.SS Packed disks
When \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] is found, the export compresses each private
disk and each image in the qcow2 format:
.IP
.EX
qemu\-img convert \-p \-O qcow2 \-m 8 \-W \(rs
    \-c \-o compression_type=zstd \(rs
    [\-B backing \-F backing\-format] disk packed\-disk
.EE
.PP
with zlib, without \f[B]\-o compression_type=zstd\f[R], when QEMU is
older than 5.1.
A private disk keeps its backing file, the image, as it is.
A packed disk is a valid qcow2 file, that QEMU runs as it is.
The member is marked \f[B]packed\f[R] in \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], with
its \f[B]real_size\f[R].
A disk or an image in another format, as raw, is stored as it is; so is
a disk that \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] can\(aqt read, as when its image is
gone, and the export reports it as a warning.
.PP
The import converts the packed members back to normal qcow2 files, the
private disks on top of their new image.
When \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] isn\(aqt found, or the image of a private disk
is left unset, the disk stays compressed and the import reports it: the
disk works as it is.
.PP
The \f[B]real_size\f[R] also lets the import recognize an image that
this computer already has: a file of the image library with the same
name and size is used instead of a copy.
.SS Holes
Disks are sparse files: a disk of 20 GB can take 2 GB of space.
The archive keeps their holes, as GNU sparse 1.0 members, written by
\f[B]bsdtar\f[R], by GNU \f[B]tar\f[R] with \f[B]\-\-sparse\f[R], or by
Virtualbricks itself when neither is installed.
A member larger than 8 GiB written by Virtualbricks has its size in
base\-256 in its tar header, not in a pax record, which Python\(aqs
\f[B]tarfile\f[R] would read wrong (see \f[B]BUGS\f[R]).
.PP
When the archive is extracted, \f[B]bsdtar\f[R] turns the runs of zeros
into holes itself, with \f[B]\-S\f[R].
After GNU \f[B]tar\f[R] or \f[B]tarfile\f[R], the import does it for the
private disks and the copied images: blocks of 64 KiB of zeros become
holes if they add up to 1 MiB at least.
.SS Older archives
The archives of older versions of Virtualbricks have no
\f[B]contents.toml\f[R]; most are compressed with gzip, and some hold
the project file of Virtualbricks 2.1, \f[B].project\f[R].
They are read as the new ones, but an inspection has to read an archive
without \f[B]contents.toml\f[R] to its end to know what it holds.
It sends what it knows, the \f[I]head\f[R], as soon as it has read the
project file, and the full list of members at the end.
.SH THE PROCESS
.SS Starting
The application starts the process with its own Python interpreter and
its own environment:
.IP
.EX
python \-m virtualbricks.config.archive
.EE
.PP
The process lowers its priority to \f[B]nice\f[R] 10, and handles
SIGTERM.
.PP
The process always comes from the same installation as the application,
so the protocol has no version: it changes with the code, and this page
with it.
.SS The job
The process reads its standard input to the end, then parses it as a
TOML table.
The key \f[B]job\f[R] says which job it is, \f[B]inspect\f[R],
\f[B]export\f[R] or \f[B]import\f[R]; the other keys depend on the job,
and are described in \f[B]JOBS\f[R].
.IP
.EX
job = \(dqinspect\(dq
archive = \(dq/home/user/lab.vbp\(dq
.EE
.PP
An unknown job is an error, as the other errors of the job.
A missing key, or one of the wrong type, is a bug of the caller: the
process reports it with its traceback.
.SS Messages
The process writes to its standard output one JSON object a line, and
flushes each one.
Every object has one key, which says what it is:
.TP
\f[B]progress\f[R]
{\(dqstep\(dq: \f[I]step\f[R], \(dqdone\(dq: \f[I]bytes\f[R],
\(dqtotal\(dq: \f[I]bytes\f[R]}: how far a step of the job has gone; see
\f[B]PROGRESS\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]created\f[R]
\f[I]path\f[R]: the process created a file or a folder outside the
job\(aqs folder, which it removes if the job doesn\(aqt end well; see
\f[B]Cleaning up\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]head\f[R]
\f[I]contents\f[R]: what an inspection knows of an older archive before
reaching its end; see \f[B]inspect\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]result\f[R]
\f[I]result\f[R]: the job is done.
It depends on the job.
.TP
\f[B]error\f[R]
\f[I]message\f[R]: the job failed.
.PP
The messages come in this order:
.IP
.EX
messages = *( progress / created / head ) [ result / error ]
.EE
.PP
\f[B]result\f[R] or \f[B]error\f[R] is the last message; there is none
when the job is cancelled or the process dies.
A reader ignores anything else: an empty line, a line that isn\(aqt
JSON, a value that isn\(aqt an object, an object with none of these
keys.
.PP
An import, for one, says:
.IP
.EX
 application                                  process
   |                                            |
   |  job = \(dqimport\(dq ... EOF                    |
   |\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|
   |                                            | extract
   |  {\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqextract\(dq, ...}}    |
   |<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
   |  {\(dqcreated\(dq: \(dq.../vimages/debian.qcow2\(dq}   | copy
   |<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
   |  {\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqunpack\(dq, ...}}     | unpack
   |<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
   |  {\(dqresult\(dq: {\(dqname\(dq: \(dqlab\(dq, ...}}          |
   |<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
   |  exit status 0                             |
   |<\- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \-|
.EE
.SS Ending
The process exits when its job ends, with:
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
The job is done: the last message is \f[B]result\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
SIGTERM cancelled the job.
The process removed what it wrote, and sent nothing more.
.TP
\f[B]2\f[R]
The job failed: the last message is \f[B]error\f[R], with a message for
the user, as \(dqthe archive has no project file\(dq or the error of
\f[B]bsdtar\f[R] or \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]3\f[R]
A bug: the last message is \f[B]error\f[R], with the Python traceback.
.PP
A process killed by another signal sends nothing more, and may leave
files behind: see \f[B]Cleaning up\f[R].
The messages tell the outcome, not the exit status: a job is done when
\f[B]result\f[R] came.
.SS Cancelling
SIGTERM stops the job wherever it is.
The process stops its \f[B]tar\f[R] or \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R], removes the
files it wrote, and exits with 1:
.IP \(bu 2
An export removes its unfinished archive and its folder.
An archive that was already there under the same name is left as it was.
.IP \(bu 2
An import removes its folder in the workspace and the images it copied
to the image library.
.IP \(bu 2
An inspection writes nothing.
.SS Cleaning up
A job writes its files where they end up, under a temporary name, and
renames them at the end, so that a job that doesn\(aqt end leaves
nothing behind.
When the job fails or is cancelled, the process removes them.
When the process dies before it can, the application removes them: the
job\(aqs folder, when it made it, and every path that the process sent
with \f[B]created\f[R].
.TP
\&.\f[I]name\f[R].vbp.\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R].part, .virtualbricks\-export\-\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R]/
The unfinished archive and its folder, next to the archive, which the
export makes and sends with \f[B]created\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/.importing\-\f[I]name\f[R]\-\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R]/
The folder of an import, which the application makes before it starts
the process.
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/vimages/\f[I]file\f[R]
An image that the import copied to the library, and sent with
\f[B]created\f[R].
.PP
The application removes them only when the process ended without a
\f[B]result\f[R].
.SH JOBS
.SS inspect
Read what an archive holds: its project file, its README and the list of
its members.
.TP
\f[B]archive\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
The archive to read.
.PP
The result is an object:
.TP
\f[B]path\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
The archive.
.TP
\f[B]data\f[R] = \f[I]object\f[R]
The project file, in the current format: converted from
\f[B].project\f[R], or upgraded from an older \f[B]project.toml\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]description\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
The README, or \(dq\(dq when there is none.
Bytes that aren\(aqt UTF\-8 are replaced.
.TP
\f[B]members\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R]
[\f[I]name\f[R], \f[I]size\f[R], \f[I]kind\f[R], \f[I]packed\f[R],
\f[I]real_size\f[R]] for each member, in the order of the archive: from
\f[B]contents.toml\f[R] when the archive has one, otherwise from the tar
headers, with \f[I]packed\f[R] false and \f[I]real_size\f[R] 0.
.TP
\f[B]complete\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R]
Whether \f[B]members\f[R] lists every member of the archive.
.TP
\f[B]report\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R]
[\f[I]level\f[R], \f[I]text\f[R], \f[I]where\f[R]] for each problem
found in the project file; \f[I]level\f[R] is \f[B]info\f[R],
\f[B]warning\f[R] or \f[B]error\f[R], \f[I]where\f[R] the key it\(aqs
about, or \(dq\(dq.
.TP
\f[B]converted\f[R] = \f[I]boolean\f[R]
Whether \f[B]data\f[R] was converted from \f[B].project\f[R].
.PP
An inspection sends the progress of the step \f[B]read\f[R].
A new archive is read up to the end of its head: the result comes with
\f[B]complete\f[R] true, and the progress stops early.
An archive without \f[B]contents.toml\f[R] is read to its end; as soon
as its project file is read, the inspection sends a \f[B]head\f[R]
message, an object as the result with \f[B]complete\f[R] false and the
members read so far.
The Import window shows its form from the head, and completes it with
the result.
.IP
.EX
  new archive, with contents.toml
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+ \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- +
  | contents | project | README |   disks, images...      |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+ \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- \- +
  \(aq\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- read \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\(aq  not read
                                \(aq\-> result, complete

  older archive, without contents.toml
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  |   ...   | project |   README, disks, images...         |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  \(aq\-\-\-\-\-\- read \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\(aq\-> head, not complete
  \(aq\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- read \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\(aq
                                        result, complete <\-\(aq
.EE
.PP
The inspection fails when the archive has no project file, when the
project file can\(aqt be read or converted, or when
\f[B]contents.toml\f[R] comes from a newer Virtualbricks.
.SS export
Write an archive of a project:
.IP
.EX
job = \(dqexport\(dq
project = \(dq/home/user/.virtualbricks/lab\(dq
output = \(dq/home/user/lab.vbp\(dq
files = [\(dqproject.toml\(dq, \(dqREADME.md\(dq, \(dqvm1_hda.cow\(dq]
images = [[\(dqdebian\(dq, \(dq/srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2\(dq]]
compression = \(dqnone\(dq
qemu_img = \(dq/usr/bin/qemu\-img\(dq
.EE
.TP
\f[B]project\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
The folder of the project.
.TP
\f[B]output\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
The archive to write.
A file with that name is replaced when the export ends.
.TP
\f[B]files\f[R] = \f[I]array of strings\f[R]
The files of the project to store, relative to its folder.
The Export window always stores \f[B]project.toml\f[R] and the README,
\f[B]README.md\f[R] or, in a project not opened since 3.0,
\f[B]README\f[R], and lets you choose the private disks, the other files
and the images; it never stores what older versions left in the folder:
the \f[I].images\f[R] folder and the \f[I].project\f[R] files.
.TP
\f[B]images\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R] of [\f[I]name\f[R], \f[I]path\f[R]]
The images to store, as \f[I].images/name\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]compression\f[R] = \f[B]\(dqgzip\(dq\f[R] | \f[B]\(dqnone\(dq\f[R], default \f[B]\(dqgzip\(dq\f[R]
Whether to compress the archive with gzip.
The application asks for \f[B]\(dqnone\(dq\f[R] when it gives
\f[B]qemu_img\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]qemu_img\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R], default \f[B]\(dq\(dq\f[R]
The \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] that packs the disks, or \(dq\(dq not to pack
them.
.PP
The export takes these steps:
.IP "1." 3
It creates the unfinished archive,
\&.\f[I]name\f[R].vbp.\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R].part, and a folder,
\&.virtualbricks\-export\-\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R], both next to the archive
because the packed disks can be large, and sends them with
\f[B]created\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
It puts in the folder each member under its name in the archive: a qcow2
disk or image packed by \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R], in the step \f[B]pack\f[R],
and any other file as a symbolic link to it.
.IP "3." 3
It writes \f[B]contents.toml\f[R] in the folder.
.IP "4." 3
It writes the folder into the unfinished archive with the archive tool,
following the links, through gzip if asked, in the step \f[B]write\f[R].
.IP "5." 3
It renames the unfinished archive to \f[I]output\f[R], and removes the
folder.
.IP
.EX
 lab/project.toml  \-\- link \-\->  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
 lab/README.md     \-\- link \-\->  | .virtualbricks\-export\-*/  |
 lab/vm1_hda.cow   \-\- pack \-\->  |   contents.toml           |
 debian\-12.qcow2   \-\- pack \-\->  |   project.toml  README.md |
                                |   vm1_hda.cow             |
                                |   .images/debian          |
                                +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
                                     | tar, in order
                                     v
                                .lab.vbp.XXXXXXXX.part
                                     | rename, at the end
                                     v
                                lab.vbp
.EE
.PP
The result is an object:
.TP
\f[B]output\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
The archive.
.TP
\f[B]size\f[R] = \f[I]integer\f[R]
Its size, in bytes.
.TP
\f[B]report\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R]
[\f[I]level\f[R], \f[I]text\f[R], \f[I]where\f[R]] for each problem, as
a disk stored as it is.
.SS import
Make a project of an archive.
The application reads the archive with \f[B]inspect\f[R] first, and asks
the user what to do with each image; this job does the rest:
.IP
.EX
job = \(dqimport\(dq
archive = \(dq/home/user/lab.vbp\(dq
staging = \(dq/home/user/.virtualbricks/.importing\-lab\-k2x9q1ab\(dq
destination = \(dq/home/user/.virtualbricks/lab\(dq
qemu_img = \(dq/usr/bin/qemu\-img\(dq

[[images]]
name = \(dqdebian\(dq
choice = \(dqcopy\(dq
path = \(dq/home/user/.virtualbricks/vimages/debian\-12.qcow2\(dq
fallback = \(dq\(dq

[settings]
qemupath = \(dq/usr/bin\(dq
.EE
.TP
\f[B]archive\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
The archive to import.
.TP
\f[B]staging\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
An empty folder of the workspace,
\&.importing\-\f[I]name\f[R]\-\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R], that the application
creates and the list of projects ignores.
The archive is extracted there.
.TP
\f[B]destination\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
The folder of the new project, \f[I]workspace\f[R]/\f[I]name\f[R].
If a folder with that name appeared meanwhile, the import takes the next
free name, \f[I]name\f[R]\-2, \f[I]name\f[R]\-3...
and says so in its report.
An import never replaces a project.
.TP
\f[B]images\f[R] = \f[I]array of tables\f[R]
What to do with each image of the project file:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
The name of the image in the project file.
.TP
\f[B]choice\f[R] = \f[B]\(dqcopy\(dq\f[R] | \f[B]\(dquse\(dq\f[R] | \f[B]\(dqskip\(dq\f[R] | \f[B]\(dqauto\(dq\f[R]
\f[B]copy\f[R] moves the image from the archive to \f[B]path\f[R], or to
the next free name, \f[I]debian\-12.1.qcow2\f[R],
\f[I]debian\-12.2.qcow2\f[R]...
\f[B]use\f[R] uses the file \f[B]path\f[R] of this computer.
\f[B]skip\f[R] leaves the image unset: its disks get one in their
settings later.
\f[B]auto\f[R] copies the image if the archive has it, otherwise uses
\f[B]fallback\f[R], otherwise skips it; the application sends it for the
images of an older archive that the inspection hadn\(aqt seen yet when
the import started.
.TP
\f[B]path\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
Where the copy goes, or the file used.
.TP
\f[B]fallback\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
For \f[B]auto\f[R]: the file to use if the archive doesn\(aqt have the
image, or \(dq\(dq.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]settings\f[R] = \f[I]table\f[R]
The settings of the project to replace with this computer\(aqs:
\f[B]qemupath\f[R] and \f[B]vdepath\f[R], the folders of QEMU and VDE.
.TP
\f[B]qemu_img\f[R] = \f[I]path\f[R]
The \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] that unpacks and rebases the disks, or \(dq\(dq.
.PP
The application gives every image a default.
An image that the archive has is copied into the image library,
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/vimages, unless the library has a file with the same
file name and size, which is used.
An image that the archive doesn\(aqt have uses its file if this computer
has it at the same path, or a file of the library with its name, and is
left unset otherwise.
A setting is replaced, unless you say otherwise, when its folder
doesn\(aqt exist on this computer.
.PP
The import takes these steps:
.IP
.EX
 workspace/
 |
 +\-\- .importing\-lab\-k2x9q1ab/   1  extract the archive here
 |     contents.toml            2  note what\(aqs packed, remove
 |     project.toml             3  read, or convert .project
 |     README.md
 |     .images/debian  \-\-\-.     4  copy, use or skip images
 |     vm1_hda.cow        |     5  rewrite project.toml
 |                        |     6  rebase \-u the private
 |                        |        disks on their images
 +\-\- vimages/             |     7  unpack the packed disks
 |     debian\-12.qcow2 <\-\-\(aq     8  turn zeros into holes
 |
 +\-\- lab/  <\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-  9  rename into place
.EE
.IP "1." 3
Extract the archive into \f[B]staging\f[R], in the step
\f[B]extract\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
Read which members are packed from \f[B]contents.toml\f[R], and remove
it: it isn\(aqt a file of the project.
Rename \f[B]README\f[R], the README of before 3.0, to
\f[B]README.md\f[R], unless there is one.
.IP "3." 3
Read the project file and upgrade it, or convert \f[B].project\f[R].
.IP "4." 3
Move each copied image to the library, sending it with
\f[B]created\f[R]; then remove the \f[I].images\f[R] folder with the
images that aren\(aqt copied.
.IP "5." 3
Rewrite \f[B]project.toml\f[R] with the path of each image, \(dq\(dq for
an image left unset, and with \f[B]settings\f[R].
.IP "6." 3
Point each private disk at its image, in the format of the image:
\f[B]qemu\-img rebase \-u \-b\f[R] \f[I]image\f[R] \f[B]\-F\f[R]
\f[I]format\f[R] \f[I]disk\f[R].
.IP "7." 3
Unpack the packed disks and images, in the step \f[B]unpack\f[R].
.IP "8." 3
After GNU \f[B]tar\f[R] or \f[B]tarfile\f[R], turn the runs of zeros of
the private disks and of the copied images into holes.
.IP "9." 3
Rename \f[B]staging\f[R] to the project\(aqs folder, with the
permissions of a normal folder.
.PP
The result is an object:
.TP
\f[B]name\f[R] = \f[I]string\f[R]
The name of the new project: the name of \f[B]destination\f[R], or the
next free one.
.TP
\f[B]report\f[R] = \f[I]array\f[R]
[\f[I]level\f[R], \f[I]text\f[R], \f[I]where\f[R]] for each problem: an
image left unset, a disk left compressed or not rebased, what the
conversion of \f[B].project\f[R] found.
.SH PROGRESS
Each step counts bytes, \f[I]done\f[R] of \f[I]total\f[R]:
.TP
\f[B]read\f[R], in \f[B]inspect\f[R]
The bytes of the archive read, of its size.
.TP
\f[B]pack\f[R], in \f[B]export\f[R]
The bytes of the disks and images packed, of their data without holes.
.TP
\f[B]write\f[R], in \f[B]export\f[R]
The bytes of the tar written, before gzip, of the data of the members
without holes.
.TP
\f[B]extract\f[R], in \f[B]import\f[R]
The bytes of the archive given to the archive tool, of its size.
.TP
\f[B]unpack\f[R], in \f[B]import\f[R]
The bytes of the packed disks and images unpacked, of their size.
.PP
A step sends its progress at most ten times a second, and when it ends;
a step with nothing to do sends nothing.
The steps of \f[B]pack\f[R] and \f[B]unpack\f[R] follow the percentage
that \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R] prints.
The \f[I]done\f[R] of a step can stay below its \f[I]total\f[R], as when
an inspection stops at the head of an archive, or go past it, as in
\f[B]write\f[R], which counts the tar headers too: a progress bar shows
the smaller of the two.
.SH ARCHIVE TOOLS
The process runs the first archive tool it finds: \f[B]bsdtar\f[R];
otherwise \f[B]tar\f[R], if the first line of \f[B]tar \-\-version\f[R]
says it\(aqs GNU tar, as the \f[B]tar\f[R] of BusyBox doesn\(aqt keep
holes; otherwise Python\(aqs \f[B]tarfile\f[R].
.TP
Reading the head
Always with \f[B]tarfile\f[R], reading the archive as a stream.
If \f[B]tarfile\f[R] finds a member that it would read wrong (see
\f[B]BUGS\f[R]), the process lists the archive with the tool instead,
\f[B]bsdtar \-t \-v \-f\f[R] \f[I]archive\f[R] or \f[B]tar \-t \-v
\-\-quoting\-style=literal \-f\f[R] \f[I]archive\f[R], and extracts the
files of the head with \f[B]\-x \-O \-f\f[R] \f[I]archive\f[R]
\f[I]member\f[R].
.TP
Writing
\f[B]bsdtar \-c \-L \-\-format pax \-f \- \-C\f[R] \f[I]folder\f[R]
\f[B]\-\-\f[R] \f[I]members\f[R], or \f[B]tar \-c \-\-sparse
\-\-dereference \-\-format=posix \-f \- \-C\f[R] \f[I]folder\f[R]
\f[B]\-\-\f[R] \f[I]members\f[R], or \f[B]tarfile\f[R] with the sparse
members written by Virtualbricks.
The process compresses the output with gzip itself, so the archive is
the same whatever the tool.
.TP
Extracting
The process feeds the archive to the tool through a pipe, so that the
progress is the same with every tool: \f[B]bsdtar \-x \-S \-f \-
\-C\f[R] \f[I]folder\f[R], or \f[B]tar \-x \-f \- \-C\f[R]
\f[I]folder\f[R] with \f[B]\-\-gzip\f[R], \f[B]\-\-bzip2\f[R],
\f[B]\-\-xz\f[R] or \f[B]\-\-zstd\f[R], as GNU tar doesn\(aqt recognize
the compression of a pipe.
With \f[B]tarfile\f[R], members with an absolute name, with \f[I]..\f[R]
in their name, links and devices are skipped.
.SH THE APPLICATION\(aqS SIDE
The module \f[I]virtualbricks/config/archive.py\f[R] has both sides of
the protocol.
In the application, an \f[B]ArchiveJob\f[R] runs a job:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]start\f[R](\f[I]reactor\f[R]) starts the process with
\f[B]reactor.spawnProcess\f[R], writes the job as TOML and closes the
standard input.
.IP \(bu 2
A \f[B]progress\f[R] message calls \f[I]on_progress\f[R](\f[I]step\f[R],
\f[I]done\f[R], \f[I]total\f[R]), a \f[B]head\f[R] calls
\f[I]on_head\f[R](\f[I]table\f[R]), and a \f[B]created\f[R] adds its
path to the leftovers.
.IP \(bu 2
When the process ends, the Deferred \f[B]done\f[R] fires with the
result, if one came, whatever the exit status.
Otherwise the leftovers are removed, and \f[B]done\f[R] fails with
\f[B]ArchiveCancelled\f[R] after \f[B]cancel\f[R](), or else with
\f[B]ArchiveError\f[R], whose message is the \f[B]error\f[R] message, or
the standard error of the process, or \(dqthe process stopped\(dq.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]cancel\f[R]() sends SIGTERM to the process.
.PP
Three functions make the jobs, the first two in
\f[B]virtualbricks.config.archive\f[R] and the last in
\f[B]virtualbricks.config.importing\f[R], and \f[B]done\f[R] fires with
what they make of the result:
.TP
\f[B]inspect_archive\f[R](\f[I]path\f[R], \f[I]on_head\f[R], \f[I]on_progress\f[R])
Fires with an \f[B]ArchiveContents\f[R]; \f[I]on_head\f[R] gets one too.
.TP
\f[B]export_project\f[R](\f[I]project\f[R], \f[I]output\f[R], \f[I]files\f[R], \f[I]images\f[R], \f[I]on_progress\f[R], \f[I]qemu_img\f[R])
Fires with the result object.
Asks for \f[B]\(dqnone\(dq\f[R] compression when \f[I]qemu_img\f[R] is
given.
.TP
\f[B]import_project\f[R](\f[I]plan\f[R], \f[I]workspace\f[R], \f[I]on_progress\f[R], \f[I]qemu_img\f[R])
Creates the \f[B]staging\f[R] folder, a leftover from the start, and
fires with an \f[B]ImportResult\f[R], its name and its report.
\f[B]plan_import\f[R] makes the \f[I]plan\f[R], with the default of
every choice, from an \f[B]ArchiveContents\f[R]; \f[B]update_plan\f[R]
settles it with the result of the inspection when the head came first.
.PP
The Import window runs an inspection as soon as an archive is chosen,
and cancels it when another is chosen or when the import starts, which
reads the whole archive anyway.
Closing the Import window cancels its inspection, but closing either
window lets an import or an export go on to its end, and the application
logs its result.
.IP
.EX
from virtualbricks.config.archive import inspect_archive

def show(contents):
    print(contents.description)
    for member in contents.members:
        print(member.kind, member.name, member.size)

job = inspect_archive(
    \(dq/home/user/lab.vbp\(dq,
    on_progress=lambda step, done, total: print(step, done),
)
job.done.addCallback(show)
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]name\f[R].vbp
An archive.
.TP
\&.\f[I]name\f[R].vbp.\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R].part
An archive being written, next to it.
.TP
\&.virtualbricks\-export\-\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R]/
The members of an archive being written, next to it.
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/.importing\-\f[I]name\f[R]\-\f[I]XXXXXXXX\f[R]/
A project being imported.
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/vimages/
The image library, where the import copies the images.
.TP
\f[I]workspace\f[R]/\f[I]name\f[R]/
An imported project.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the process by hand.
Inspect an archive:
.IP
.EX
$ printf \(aqjob = \(dqinspect\(dq\(rsnarchive = \(dq/home/user/lab.vbp\(dq\(rsn\(aq |
>     python \-m virtualbricks.config.archive
{\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqread\(dq, \(dqdone\(dq: 512, \(dqtotal\(dq: 409600}}
{\(dqresult\(dq: {\(dqpath\(dq: \(dq/home/user/lab.vbp\(dq, \(dqdata\(dq: ...}}
.EE
.PP
The result, laid out:
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqpath\(dq: \(dq/home/user/lab.vbp\(dq,
  \(dqdata\(dq: {
    \(dqformat\(dq: 1,
    \(dqimages\(dq: {
      \(dqdebian\(dq: {\(dqpath\(dq: \(dq/srv/vm/debian\-12.qcow2\(dq, ...}
    },
    \(dqbricks\(dq: {\(dqvm1\(dq: {\(dqtype\(dq: \(dqqemu\(dq, ...}}
  },
  \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqA two\-host lab.\(rsn\(dq,
  \(dqmembers\(dq: [
    [\(dqproject.toml\(dq, 255, \(dqproject\(dq, false, 0],
    [\(dqREADME.md\(dq, 16, \(dqreadme\(dq, false, 0],
    [\(dqvm1_hda.cow\(dq, 197120, \(dqdisk\(dq, true, 196616],
    [\(dq.images/debian\(dq, 197120, \(dqimage\(dq, true, 196616]
  ],
  \(dqcomplete\(dq: true,
  \(dqreport\(dq: [],
  \(dqconverted\(dq: false
}
.EE
.PP
Export a project, with the job of \f[B]export\f[R] in
\f[I]export.toml\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
$ python \-m virtualbricks.config.archive < export.toml
{\(dqcreated\(dq: \(dq/home/user/.lab.vbp.hsp68v_7.part\(dq}
{\(dqcreated\(dq: \(dq/home/user/.virtualbricks\-export\-chcr2_tb\(dq}
{\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqpack\(dq, \(dqdone\(dq: 0, \(dqtotal\(dq: 393232}}
{\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqpack\(dq, \(dqdone\(dq: 393232, \(dqtotal\(dq: 393232}}
{\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqwrite\(dq, \(dqdone\(dq: 409600, \(dqtotal\(dq: 394772}}
{\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqwrite\(dq, \(dqdone\(dq: 394772, \(dqtotal\(dq: 394772}}
{\(dqresult\(dq: {\(dqoutput\(dq: \(dq/home/user/lab.vbp\(dq, \(dqsize\(dq: 409600, ...}}
$ echo $?
0
.EE
.PP
Stop a long export: the process removes the unfinished archive and its
folder.
.IP
.EX
$ python \-m virtualbricks.config.archive \(rs
>     < export.toml > messages &
$ sleep 2; kill $!; wait $!; echo $?
1
$ tail \-n 1 messages
{\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqwrite\(dq, \(dqdone\(dq: 48234496, ...}}
.EE
.PP
A job that fails:
.IP
.EX
$ printf \(aqjob = \(dqinspect\(dq\(rsnarchive = \(dqnotes.txt\(dq\(rsn\(aq |
>     python \-m virtualbricks.config.archive
{\(dqprogress\(dq: {\(dqstep\(dq: \(dqread\(dq, \(dqdone\(dq: 512, \(dqtotal\(dq: 5383}}
{\(dqerror\(dq: \(dqnotes.txt: invalid header\(dq}
$ echo $?
2
.EE
.SH BUGS
An inspection that has to read the archive with the tool sends no
progress, and no head.
.PP
Python\(aqs \f[B]tarfile\f[R], from 3.10 to 3.13, misreads a GNU sparse
1.0 member whose size is in a pax record, as \f[B]bsdtar\f[R] and GNU
\f[B]tar\f[R] write the members larger than 8 GiB, and loses the member
after it.
The process then reads the archive with the tool; without
\f[B]bsdtar\f[R] or GNU \f[B]tar\f[R], it can\(aqt read it, and says
\(dqinstall bsdtar or GNU tar to read this archive\(dq.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5), \f[B]bsdtar\f[R](1),
\f[B]tar\f[R](1), \f[B]tar\f[R](5), \f[B]qemu\-img\f[R](1)
.PP
